      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We can take advantage of a useful property which relates multiplication by the reciprocal in one equation by choosing two numbers which have opposite signs.  In this problem, the opposite of $-11$ is $11$ and the opposite of $-12$ is $12$.  Therefore, we multiply equation $1$ by $11$ and equation $2$ by $5$ to obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n-33x+55y&=-121,\\\\\n35x+10y&=-60.\n\\end{align*}We then add the equations to eliminate $x$,\n\\begin{align*}\n(-33x+35x+55y+10y) &=-121-60,\\\\\n2x+65y &=-181.\\\\\n\\intertext{Also, note that the coefficient of $y$ in the new equation is $11(1-5)$ instead of $5(3+7)$.  Hence, these two can be multiplied by $11$ and $5$, respectively, to provide a multiplication factor of 115.  This can be verified with the expression}\n(-715x-682y) &= -2015.\\\\\n-15x+15y&=47.\n\\end{align*}We then subtract to eliminate $x$ one more time to obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n(-15x+15y-615x+641y) &= 47-2015,\\\\\n-630y &=-1968.\\\\\ny= \\tfrac{1968}{630}, &= \\tfrac{312}{97}\n\\end{align*}Finally, we substitute back in either of the original equations.  The hyperbola around $x=30$ can be sketched in the picture and shows that $1$ unit works; therefore, $(x,y) =\n(-\\tfrac{2015}{630},\\tfrac{312}{97})$, which is equivalent to $\\boxed{(-\\tfrac{11}{3},\\tfrac{312}{97})}$.\nasymptote of the hyperbola suggest $y$ to be rational\ntherefore $30 y-30 \\bmid 11 x-11$ and $12 y-12 \\bmid -12 x+12$\nthe line $y=\\frac{312}{97}$ works for both, hence $x-\\frac{2015+312}{97} = -\\frac{11\\cdot 97 - 312\\cdot 30}{3\\cdot 97} = \\frac{970}{3\\cdot 97} = \\frac{100}{3}=-\\frac{11}{3}$",
