      "text": "To solve the system of equations\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11, \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n7x + 2y = -12, \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\nwe can use the method of elimination. The goal is to eliminate one of the variables, either \\(x\\) or \\(y\\), by making the coefficients of one of the variables the same in both equations and then subtracting or adding the equations to eliminate that variable.\n\nFirst, we will eliminate \\(y\\). To do this, we need to make the coefficients of \\(y\\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. We can achieve this by multiplying equation (1) by 2 and equation (2) by 5:\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22, \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60, \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add equations (3) and (4) to eliminate \\(y\\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60)\n\\]\n\\[\n6x + 35x = -82\n\\]\n\\[\n41x = -82\n\\]\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{82}{41}\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = -\\frac{82}{41}\\) back into equation (1) to solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3\\left(-\\frac{82}{41}\\right) - 5y = -11\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{246}{41} - 5y = -11\n\\]\nTo clear the fraction, we multiply every term by 41:\n\\[\n-246 - 205y = -451\n\\]\n\\[\n-205y = -451 + 246\n\\]\n\\[\n-205y = -205\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n(x, y) = \\left(-\\frac{82}{41}, 1\\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(-\\frac{82}{41}, 1\\right)}\n\\]",
